[03:21] CANDIDATE: Reading it. So sessions are nodes, each prerequisite pair is a directed edge, and I need a valid ordering, so this is a topological sort. What should I return if there's a cycle, and can there be duplicate pairs?
[03:50] INTERVIEWER: There's no self-loops, and all pairs are distinct, so duplicates aren't a concern. For the cycle case, a valid full ordering won't exist — you should return an empty list. So, what's your approach here?
    [item=core-1; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=677ms wall=0.9s]
<<READY 03:50>>
[03:50] CANDIDATE: Okay. I'll use Kahn's algorithm. Build an adjacency list and an in-degree array, push every zero in-degree session into a queue, pop one, append it to the order, and decrement its neighbours. If the order ends up shorter than n there's a cycle, so I return empty. That's O of n plus e time and space.
[04:25] INTERVIEWER: That's the right shape. One thing to be careful about — when you build the adjacency list from a pair, which direction does the edge point? Convince me with a quick example.
    [item=core-1; model=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash first_token=690ms wall=0.8s]
<<READY 04:25>>
[04:25] CANDIDATE: A pair is a course and its prerequisite, so the edge points from the prerequisite to the course. For pairs 1 and 0, edge from 0 to 1, in-degree of 1 goes up, and 0 comes out first. Let me write it.
[04:46] INTERVIEWER: Good, that's the correct direction. Go ahead and write it.
